        "Cetane number",
        "Ignition system"
      ],
      "abstract": "Abstract Levulinic acid is an energetic molecule produced by the hydrolysis of cellulose and considered to be a viable biorenewable diesel fuel additive to offset carbon emissions. Esterification of levulinic acid with various chain length alcohols yields levulinate esters. Esterification with long-chain alcohols can result in levulinate esters which are miscible with diesel fuel and yield suitable physicochemical properties for compression ignition engines. Additionally, levulinate esters have been shown to offer favorable techno-economics and suitability across a range of engine platforms. Despite this promise, no systematic investigation of the effects of alkyl chain length on the resulting fuel properties or diesel engine performance has been performed. The current research provides the first comparative study of the fuel properties and combustion dynamics of levulinate esters with variable alkyl chain length. Methyl, ethyl, butyl, hexyl and octyl levulinate esters were created in the laboratory (purity >94%) and blended with ultra-low sulfur diesel at 20% (vol./vol). Butanol is shown to be a necessary co-solvent to ensure short chain levulinate ester miscibility, principall...",
